{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794352365599,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7bc4004001KpqMrL7FYPkEsY","messageID":"msg_1c7bbd027001pBjdjLq3KCPLLh","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794352365600,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7bc4007001foQkZyOg0CHuME","messageID":"msg_1c7bbd027001pBjdjLq3KCPLLh","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"text","text":"<think>The API works as designed. A couple of observations:\n\n1. `list range datetime` returned empty: start_date=2024-05-04T11:00:00Z, end_date=2024-05-04T12:00:00Z — the event at 10:00 starts before the range, so it's excluded. Correct per my start_time-based filtering.\n\n2. The error message for a bad start_date query param says \"start date must be a valid ISO-8601 date-time\" — the field label. Fine, though \"start_date\" would be clearer. Let me improve parse_boundary to give a clearer message for date-only parse failures.\n\nActually, in parse_boundary, when len(text)==10 but strptime fails → raises ValueError → caught → \"start_date must be a valid ISO date or date-time.\" Good. But when text is longer and fromisoformat fails → parse_datetime raises ValidationError, which is NOT caught by `except ValueError` — wait, ValidationError extends Exception, not ValueError, so it propagates out of parse_boundary → caught by the caller `except ValidationError` in list_events → returns 400 with the message \"start date must be a valid ISO-8601 date-time...\". That's acceptable.\n\n3. `created_by` for Bob's edit remains \"alice\" — correct (creator recorded).\n\nNow, one issue: in the `list range` test, end_date=\"2024-05-31\" → end of day 23:59:59 → events on May 31 included. Good.\n\nAnother thought on `parse_boundary` for `start_date` given as a date-only: midnight UTC. Good.\n\nNow let me think again about `POST /api/auth/register` returning a token — fine.\n\nOne more consideration: the `register` response includes \"username\". Fine.\n\nLet me now double-check the 401 behavior for `GET /api/events` — it's public. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `require_user` on PUT/DELETE — the spec says \"Any signed-in user may change any event\". Good.\n\nNow let me clean up the smoke test file later (or keep it? It's a dev artifact in the repo root — I should remove it before finishing, or move it to a tests/ folder. I'll delete it at the end.)\n\nNow the frontend. Let me write the files.\n\nFirst, check the installed tailwind version and vite plugin API.\n\nFiles to write:\n1. frontend/vite.config.ts — add tailwindcss plugin\n2. frontend/src/index.css — @import \"tailwindcss\";\n3. frontend/src/main.tsx — import index.css\n4. frontend/src/types.ts\n5. frontend/src/api.ts\n6. frontend/src/lib/dates.ts\n7. frontend/src/components/... \n8. frontend/src/App.tsx\n9. frontend/index.html — title\n\nLet me think about the component structure concretely.\n\nsrc/types.ts:\n```ts\nexport interface CalendarEvent {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  description: string | null;\n  location: string | null;\n  start_time: string;\n  end_time: string;\n  created_at: string;\n  created_by: string;\n}\n\nexport type ViewMode = \"month\" | \"week\" | \"day\";\n\nexport interface Session {\n  token: string;\n  username: string;\n}\n```\n\nsrc/api.ts:\n```ts\nimport type { CalendarEvent, Session } from \"./types\";\n\nconst TOKEN_KEY = \"calendar.token\";\nconst USERNAME_KEY = \"calendar.username\";\n\nexport class ApiError extends Error {\n  status: number;\n  errors: Record<string, string>;\n  constructor(status: number, message: string, errors: Record<string,string> = {}) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = \"ApiError\";\n    this.status = status;\n    this.errors = errors;\n  }\n}\n\nexport function loadSession(): Session | null {\n  try {\n    const token = window.localStorage.getItem(TOKEN_KEY);\n    const username = window.localStorage.getItem(USERNAME_KEY);\n    if (!token || !username) return null;\n    return { token, username };\n  } catch { return null; }\n}\n\nexport function saveSession(token: string, username: string) {...}\nexport function clearSession() {...}\n\nasync function request<T>(path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<T> {\n  const headers: Record<string, string> = { Accept: \"application/json\" };\n  if (init.body !== undefined) headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"application/json\";\n  const session = loadSession();\n  if (session) headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${session.token}`;\n  const response = await fetch(`/api${path}`, { ...init, headers });\n  const text = await response.text();\n  let payload: any = null;\n  if (text) { try { payload = JSON.parse(text); } catch { payload = null; } }\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    const message = payload?.error || payload?.message || `Request failed (${response.status}).`;\n    throw new ApiError(response.status, message, payload?.errors ?? {});\n  }\n  return payload as T;\n}\n\nexport const api = {\n  async register(username: string, password: string): Promise<Session> {\n    const data = await request<{token: string; username: string}>(\"/auth/register\", {method: \"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({username, password})});\n    return { token: data.token, username: data.username };\n  },\n  async login(username, password): Promise<Session> {\n    const data = await request<{token: string}>(\"/auth/login\", {method:\"POST\", body: JSON.stringify({username, password})});\n    const username = ...; // need username — pass it in\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nFor login, the API returns only the token, so the session username comes from the form input. But the canonical casing might differ. I'll use the entered username for the session display, but after login I could call a \"me\" endpoint... not required. I'll use the entered username. Hmm — but what if the user logs in with a different case than registered? Display would show the entered case. Fine.\n\nWait — but `created_by` comes from the server's canonical username. Fine.\n\nActually, let me add a `GET /api/auth/me` endpoint returning the username for the token. Nice-to-have: after login, fetch /auth/me to get the canonical username. I'll add it — small backend addition, improves accuracy. Let me add:\n\n```python\n@app.get(\"/api/auth/me\")\n@require_user\ndef me():\n    return jsonify({\"username\": g.username})\n```\n\nThen the frontend after login: token → save session with the entered username, then optionally refresh from /auth/me. I'll do it: login → token; then GET /auth/me → username; save session. If /auth/me fails, fall back to the entered username.\n\nNow dates.ts (UTC helpers):\n\n```ts\nconst MS_DAY = 86400000;\n\nexport function parseIso(value: string): Date {\n  // API always returns Z-suffixed UTC\n  return new Date(value);\n}\n\nexport function pad(n: number): string { return n.toString().padStart(2, \"0\"); }\n\nexport function toDateKey(date: Date): string {\n  return `${date.getUTCFullYear()}-${pad(date.getUTCMonth()+1)}-${pad(date.getUTCDate())}`;\n}\n\nexport function timeKey(date: Date): string { return `${pad(date.getUTCHours())}:${pad(date.getUTCMinutes())}`; }\n\nexport function toInputValue(iso: string): string {\n  const d = new Date(iso);\n  return `${toDateKey(d)}T${timeKey(d)}`;\n}\n\nexport function fromInputValue(value: string): string {\n  // \"2024-06-01T10:00\" -> \"2024-06-01T10:00:00Z\"\n  if (!value) return \"\";\n  return value.length === 16 ? `${value}:00Z` : `${value}Z`;\n}\n\nexport function formatTime(iso: string): string {\n  const d = new Date(iso);\n  return `${pad(d.getUTCHours())}:${pad(d.getUTCMinutes())}`;\n}\n\nexport function formatLongDate(iso: string): string {\n  const d = new Date(iso);\n  return d.toLocaleDateString(\"en-GB\", { timeZone: \"UTC\", weekday: \"short\", day: \"numeric\", month: \"short\", year: \"numeric\" });\n}\n\nexport function formatRange(startIso, endIso): string\n\nexport function startOfDay(date: Date): Date\nexport function addDays(date: Date, days: number): Date\nexport function startOfWeek(date: Date): Date  // Monday\nexport function startOfMonth(date: Date): Date\nexport function addMonths(date: Date, months: number): Date\nexport function isSameDay(a: Date, b: Date): boolean\nexport function todayUtc(): Date\nexport function monthGrid(date: Date): Date[] // 42 days\n```\n\nNote: `toLocaleDateString` with timeZone: \"UTC\" is fine.\n\nNow the App layout:\n\n```\n<div className=\"min-h-screen bg-slate-100 text-slate-900\">\n  <header> ... title, view switcher, nav, auth </header>\n  <main> calendar </main>\n  modals\n</div>\n```\n\nLet me write the components:\n\n**AuthPanel** (shown when not signed in): a card with tabs \"Sign in\" / \"Create account\". Fields username/password. Submit → api.login/register → onSession callback. Show error message from API.\n\n**TopBar**: app title \"Calendar\", view switcher, prev/today/next, \"New event\" button (signed in), auth info.\n\n**MonthView**: props: days (42 Date objects), events map by day key, onSelectEvent, onSelectDay, isToday, currentMonth.\n\n**TimeGrid** (week/day): props: days (Date[]), events, onSelectEvent, onCreateAt(day, minutes).\n\n**EventDetailsModal**: props: event, signedIn, onClose, onEdit, onDelete.\n\n**EventFormModal**: props: mode, initial values, onClose, onSaved. Handles its own submit + error display.\n\n**ConfirmDialog**: props: title, message, onCancel, onConfirm, busy.\n\nLet me write these files now. I'll aim for clean Tailwind styling.\n\nColors: use indigo-600 as primary, slate for neutrals. Event chips: use a palette of colors based on a hash of the event title? Or a fixed indigo. I'll use a set of soft colors cycling by event id modulo N for visual variety.\n\nLet me define in a shared module:\n```ts\nexport const EVENT_COLORS = [\n  \"bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-800 border-indigo-200\",\n  \"bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 border-emerald-200\",\n  \"bg-amber-100 text-amber-900 border-amber-200\",\n  \"bg-rose-100 text-rose-800 border-rose-200\",\n  \"bg-sky-100 text-sky-800 border-sky-200\",\n  \"bg-violet-100 text-violet-800 border-violet-200\",\n];\nexport function colorFor(event: CalendarEvent) { return EVENT_COLORS[event.id % EVENT_COLORS.length]; }\n```\n\nNow writing. Let me be careful with TypeScript strictness — Vite's react-ts template uses strict mode; here there's no tsconfig.json in the project! Let me check: frontend/ has index.html, package.json, package-lock.json, src/, vite.config.ts. No tsconfig.json. The build script is just `vite build` — Vite handles TS via esbuild without type checking. But `npm run build` should ideally run `tsc -b && vite build`. Since there's no tsconfig, adding tsc would require adding tsconfig.json and typescript dependency. The existing setup has no typescript package at all! Let me check package.json devDependencies: @vitejs/plugin-react and vite only. And the source is .tsx. Vite transpiles TSX without type checking.\n\nI should add typescript + tsconfig.json for a proper TS project, and make the build run `tsc -b && vite build`. That requires installing typescript (@types/react, @types/react-dom too). Since npm registry access works, let me install typescript, @types/react, @types/react-dom.\n\nRisk: `tsc -b` failing on type errors would break the build. I'll make sure types are correct. Alternatively keep the build as `vite build` only (no type check) to reduce risk. But the task says \"frontend with React (TypeScript, built with Vite)\". Type-checking during build is good practice. I'll add typescript and use `tsc -b --noEmit`? `tsc -b` with noEmit isn't allowed in composite mode... Let me set up a standard Vite React TS template:\n\ntsconfig.json:\n```json\n{\n  \"files\": [],\n  \"references\": [\n    { \"path\": \"./tsconfig.app.json\" },\n    { \"path\": \"./tsconfig.node.json\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\nThat's the modern template. Simpler: single tsconfig.json with include src and \"noEmit\": true, and build script \"tsc --noEmit && vite build\". With `tsc` (not -b) and noEmit true, that works fine.\n\nI'll do:\ntsconfig.json:\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": true,\n    \"lib\": [\"ES2022\", \"DOM\", \"DOM.Iterable\"],\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"noEmit\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"noUnusedLocals\": true,\n    \"noUnusedParameters\": true,\n    \"noFallthroughCasesInSwitch\": true\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"src\", \"vite.config.ts\"]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, `include: [\"src\", \"vite.config.ts\"]` — vite.config.ts uses node types; with \"types\": not specified, it includes all @types packages. vite.config.ts imports from \"vite\" — fine. But `defineConfig` etc. is fine without node types.\n\nverbatimModuleSyntax requires `import type` for type-only imports — I'll use them.\n\nLet me install: typescript, @types/react, @types/react-dom.\n\nLet me do that now and check the versions available.\n\nThen write the frontend code.\n\nAlso, the build output: `npm run build` → `tsc --noEmit && vite build` → writes frontend/dist. Good.\n\nLet me install the depsfirst.</think>\n\nBackend works. Now let me set up the frontend dependencies:","time":{"start":1794352365576,"end":1794352365595}}}
