{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794352561536,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7bf3d6a001RxW0K50UR3yJcQ","messageID":"msg_1c7bec590001J7DCMb5SFVfmVU","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794352561536,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7bf3d6d001Rnmarvh2agpyok","messageID":"msg_1c7bec590001J7DCMb5SFVfmVU","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"text","text":"<think>As expected, browser downloads are blocked. Let me use jsdom for a render smoke test instead.\n\nPlan: install jsdom with --no-save into frontend/node_modules, write a test script that:\n1. Creates a jsdom environment with the built dist/index.html.\n2. Stubs fetch to proxy to the running server (using node's fetch and mapping /api → http://127.0.0.1:8000/api).\n3. Imports the built JS bundle and runs it.\n4. Waits, then checks the DOM for expected content (e.g., \"Event Calendar\", the auth panel, month grid).\n\nSince the built bundle is an ES module, I can import it in Node with jsdom globals set up. The bundle references `document.getElementById(\"root\")`.\n\nApproach:\n```js\nimport { JSDOM } from \"jsdom\";\nconst dom = new JSDOM(fs.readFileSync(\"dist/index.html\"), { url: \"http://localhost:8000/\", pretendToBeVisual: true });\nglobal.window = dom.window; global.document = dom.window.document; global.navigator = dom.window.navigator;\nglobal.fetch = (input, init) => { const url = new URL(input, \"http://localhost:8000\"); return fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:8000\" + url.pathname + url.search, init); };\nglobal.localStorage = dom.window.localStorage;\nawait import(\"./dist/assets/index-XXX.js\");\nawait new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1500));\nconsole.log(dom.window.document.body.textContent);\n```\n\nPotential issues: React 19 requires `window.requestAnimationFrame` (jsdom has it with pretendToBeVisual), `MessageChannel` (jsdom has it), `queueMicrotask` (node has it). Also `Element.prototype.setAttribute` etc. Should be OK.\n\nReact 19's `createRoot` requires `IS_REACT_ACT_ENVIRONMENT`? No, that's for testing-library.\n\nLet me try. jsdom install: `npm i --no-save jsdom` (won't modify package.json but will modify package-lock.json!). Hmm, `npm install --no-save` does modify package-lock.json? Actually `--no-save` prevents saving to package.json but the lockfile may still be updated. To avoid touching the lock, I can install it in a separate directory: `mkdir -p /workspace/.tmp-test && cd /workspace/.tmp-test && npm init -y && npm i jsdom`. Then run the test from there with NODE_PATH pointing to that node_modules. Since the script lives in that dir, `import { JSDOM } from \"jsdom\"` resolves locally.\n\nLet me do that. Then delete the temp dir afterwards.\n\nNote: /workspace/.tmp-test would be inside the repo — I'll delete it at the end. Also need to make sure it isn't committed (I'm not committing anyway, but let me add a .gitignore).\n\nLet me setthis up.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794352561517,"end":1794352561532}}}
