{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794352707539,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7c177be001qUkZha49S3Du62","messageID":"msg_1c7c13533001NDAzKo38eurcA0","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794352707540,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7c177c1001ymZrXPFjfOEoXQ","messageID":"msg_1c7c13533001NDAzKo38eurcA0","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"text","text":"<think>All 18 interaction checks pass. The frontend works end-to-end.\n\nNow let me:\n1. Test the Docker build (the app is built from the Dockerfile in the workspace root). Docker may not be available in the sandbox. Let me check.\n2. Verify persistence across restarts (data in ./data).\n3. Clean up temp files (smoke_test.py, tmpjsdom/, .venv, data/).\n4. Add a .gitignore.\n5. Double-check the Dockerfile works with the new frontend (npm ci requires the lock file to be in sync with package.json — I changed package.json to exact versions; the lock file was generated with ^ ranges... let me verify `npm ci` works).\n\nLet me check `npm ci` in a clean copy to make sure the lock file matches package.json.\n\nAlso important: the Dockerfile copies frontend/package.json and package-lock.json, runs npm ci, then copies the rest. My package.json now has different devDeps — the lock file must be in sync. npm install updated the lock. Let me verify with `npm ci --dry-run`.\n\nLet me also checkdocker availability.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794352707521,"end":1794352707536}}}
