{"type":"text","timestamp":1794353059739,"sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","part":{"id":"prt_1c7c6d786001Fex4E3RM4jqfyQ","messageID":"msg_1c7c6bbca001WoQheuvqzgzttM","sessionID":"ses_e3847c4f2ffeRaGpXufKYD9yDm","type":"text","text":"<think>The command hung again. The `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn\"` — hmm, the bracket trick should avoid self-matching, but the shell command line contains `[g]unicorn` which as a regex matches \"gunicorn\"... pkill matches the pattern against the full command line of processes. My own bash process command line contains the literal text `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn\"` — does the regex `[g]unicorn` match that string? The string contains \"[g]unicorn\" — the regex `[g]unicorn` matches \"gunicorn\" as a substring. Is \"gunicorn\" a substring of `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn\"`? The literal characters are `[`,`g`,`]`,`u`,`n`,`i`,`c`,`o`,`r`,`n` — \"gunicorn\" as a contiguous substring is NOT present (there's `g]unicorn`). So the bracket trick works...\n\nBut wait — the heredoc python script contains \"gunicorn\"? No.\n\nHmm, what hung? The `pkill` may have killed the gunicorn; then `setsid ... &` started a new one; sleep 3; then python. The output shows nothing at all — not even the python output. And now no gunicorn is running.\n\nMaybe the issue: the bash tool's shell session was killed by pkill matching something else. Actually! The parent bash process command line might include the whole script (bash -c '...'), which contains \"gunicorn\" from the setsid line! Yes — the bash tool runs `bash -c \"<full command>\"`, and that command text contains the literal \"gunicorn\" (in the setsid line). So `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn\"` kills the bash tool's own shell → the command never completes → timeout.\n\nSolution: avoid pkill entirely. Use a pidfile: capture the PID when starting and kill by PID. Let me restart the server and store its PID in a workspace file.\n\nLet me do it carefully:\n1. Start: `setsid ... & echo $! > /workspace/gunicorn.pid`\n2. Stop: `kill $(cat /workspace/gunicorn.pid)`\n\nSince each bash call is a separate shell, the pid file approach works.\n\nNote: with setsid, $! is the setsid PID which becomes the process group leader; killing it should kill gunicorn (setsid execs gunicorn, so the PID stays the same).\n\nLet me restart the server now.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794353059718,"end":1794353059735}}}
